WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers episode 143 coming to you on July 26th, 2026.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:11.220
Let's look at what's new across the frontiers of space and science.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.940
Three astronauts are heading home after 241 days in orbit, while Juno reaches its closest

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:18.860
approach to Earth.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:26.880
Soyuz MS-28, undocked from the International Space Station Rasvet Module at 302AM Eastern

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.640
Daylight Time.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.980
The spacecraft carried NASA astronaut Chris Williams along with Ross Cosmos Cosmonauts

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:37.240
Sergei Kud's first gov and Sergei Mikhail.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.020
The crew had spent 241 days in space before the separation.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:48.160
A four-minute 42-second de-orbit burn began at 0931 UTC.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.140
Touchdown in Kazakhstan is scheduled for 1025 UTC.

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.760
Command of the station passed to astronaut Jessica Meere before departure,

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.720
marking the end of Expedition 74, now shifting to commercial launch developments.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:07.940
SpaceX plans a tower catch attempt on the next Starship flight.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.920
Elon Musk stated the company will attempt to catch the upper stage with the launch tower on flight

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.460
14 unless post-flight data review reveals problems.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:22.500
The decision follows flight 13's successful upper stage splashdown.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.800
The booster from flight 13 already demonstrated controlled descent and landing burn performance.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.140
Tower capture would eliminate the need for down-range recovery vessels.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.120
Engineers will review telemetry from the recent flight before committing hardware to the new profile.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.140
Remember, we covered Starship Development yesterday and this step directly advances the rapid

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:49.240
reuse cadence that remains the central open question for the program.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.760
On a different note, asteroid 3 Juno reaches opposition in Aquila.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.360
The ninth magnitude asteroid becomes visible all night as it lies opposite the sun.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.560
It already sits above the horizon at sunset.

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.080
The moon lies nearby in the same constellation.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.380
Observers can track Juno throughout the night with modest telescopes.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:16.540
Opposition places the asteroid at its brightest apparent magnitude for the year.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.100
No special equipment beyond a small telescope is required for detection.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:28.300
Turning now to exoplanet atmospheres, JWST may miss water hidden deep in mini-neptunes.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:35.480
Sub-neptunes make up the most common planet type yet detected, with nearly 3,300 confirmed.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.620
Their thick, hazy atmospheres block transmission spectroscopy even with JWST.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.300
No solar system analog exists for comparison.

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:51.200
High altitude clouds or photochemical hazes are suspected to mute spectral features.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:57.580
Longer wavelength observations may eventually reveal water absorption bands that current data cannot

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:03.780
access. The cosmic spotlight looks at these JWST findings in more detail.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:12.160
JWST observations of sub-neptunes are revealing how thick, hazy atmospheres can hide water vapor even

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:19.340
when the molecules are present deeper down. The 3,300 known examples lack a solar system counterpart,

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:26.540
so models rely entirely on remote data. Transmission spectra show muted features that could indicate

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:33.420
high altitude clouds or photochemical hazes. Multi-wave length campaigns may detect water absorption

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:40.300
at longer infrared wavelengths where hazo-pacity drops. Laboratory simulations of hazy atmospheres

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.820
are now being compared directly with the telescope spectra. The next step involves stacking

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:52.240
observations across multiple transits to improve signal-to-noise at those key bands. Next up,

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.460
an exoplanet that survived its host star's death. Astronomers are examining a world that remained

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:05.260
bound after its star evolved into a white dwarf. The survival offers clues about orbital migration

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:11.840
and tidal forces during stellar expansion. Follow-up spectroscopy will search for atmospheric changes

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.260
induced by the star's final stages. The planet's current orbit lies close enough for ongoing tidal

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:23.800
heating. Its atmosphere may show traces of material accreted during the red giant phase.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.300
Shifting to another nearby system, Barnard's star planets appear inhospitable yet informative.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:36.880
The system contains multiple planets but none in the habitable zone. Researchers note the world's

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:43.480
can still constrain formation models around low mass stars. Continued radial velocity monitoring

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:50.100
aims to refine their masses and orbits. The planets orbit a red dwarf only six light years away.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.920
Their close-in paths subject them to intense stellar radiation. Moving to a rare transient event,

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:03.440
a rare blue optical transient was found in a dwarf galaxy. Chinese astronomers detected

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:11.220
AT-2019-IJN, a fast rising, slow decaying event with unusually bright radio emission.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:18.160
The transient occurred at the center of its host galaxy. Its properties do not match known supernova

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:25.180
or tidal disruption classes, suggesting a possible new explosion mechanism. The optical light curve

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:30.860
peaked within days before fading over weeks. Radio observations revealed sustained

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:37.520
high luminosity inconsistent with standard models. On the technology side, a commercial camera could

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.700
aid LIGO black hole searches. The approach would monitor auxiliary optics without requiring new

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:49.500
hardware development. It targets stray light that currently limits sensitivity at certain frequencies.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:54.640
Early tests show the method could increase detection rates for merging black holes.

00:05:55.180 --> 00:06:01.600
The camera would operate continuously alongside existing interferometers. Off-the-shelf components

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:08.500
keep costs low while addressing a known noise source. A gram-scale probe concept is also under

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:15.200
discussion for a nearby black hole. Kozimo Bambee of Fudan University outlines requirements for

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:21.400
sending a small spacecraft to a stellar mass black hole. The probe would measure strong field gravity

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:27.180
effects unreachable by remote observation. Travel time and communication constraints remain

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:33.480
the primary engineering hurdles. A gram-scale design reduces launch mass and radiation exposure

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:39.640
during the long cruise. Laser or radio links would need extreme precision over interstellar

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:46.560
distances. The cosmic deep dive examines how black holes slowly evaporate. If the sun were compressed

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.560
into a black hole, its event horizon would span only six kilometers yet contain the mass of two

00:06:52.560 --> 00:07:00.440
octillion tons. Virtual particle pairs constantly pop into existence near that horizon. One falls inward

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:07.080
while the other escapes as Hawking radiation, carrying away energy. Over time, the black hole loses

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:13.040
mass and its temperature rises, accelerating the process until the final stages release a burst of

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:20.920
particles. A solar mass black hole would require roughly 10 to the 67 years to vanish completely, far

00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:26.920
longer than the current age of the universe. The radiation spectrum follows a perfect black body

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:32.680
curve whose peak wavelength shrinks as the whole shrinks. We can calculate the spectrum of this

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:38.700
radiation in detail, but nobody knows whether the information that fell into the black hole is truly

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:45.240
destroyed or somehow encoded in the outgoing radiation. Turning to a human story from station

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:52.560
operations, Michael Foley recounts the 1997 Mir collision response. Foley describes how crewmate

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:58.900
Alexander Lazutkin accepted full responsibility for the progress collision. The crew worked to

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:04.740
stabilize the station after the impact. Foley states the team saved space station near that day.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:11.920
The account appears in the PBS series once upon a time in space. Lazutkin's decision to take blame

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:18.860
helped maintain crew cohesion during the crisis. A podcast marks the 50th anniversary of the Viking One

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:26.420
Mars landing. Episode 220 of this week in space features astrobiologist Penny Boston and planetary

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:32.880
scientist Pascal Lee. The discussion covers the first successful soft landing on Mars and its

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:41.280
scientific legacy. Viking One touched down on July 20th, 1976. The lander returned the first close-up

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:47.440
images of the Martian surface. Its biology experiments searched for signs of life in the regolith.

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:55.400
Chishiro Hayashi's 1920 birth is also remembered this week. Hayashi applied nuclear physics to stellar

00:08:55.400 --> 00:09:01.980
evolution after wartime naval service. He later became a professor at Kyoto University and developed

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:08.180
the Hayashi track for pre-main sequence stars. His work remains foundational in models of star

00:09:08.180 --> 00:09:14.760
formation. The Hayashi track describes how contracting proto-stars move across the Hertzsprung Russell

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:21.320
diagram before reaching the main sequence. His calculations incorporated realistic opacities and

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:28.560
convection that earlier models lacked. Modern stellar evolution codes still reference his 1960s papers

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:36.660
as benchmarks. A 74-year-old telescope recently captured an image of Saturn. The Soviet era AVR-1

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:43.900
instrument built in 1952 produced the photo under current amateur conditions. The result demonstrates

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:50.780
the continued usability of mid-century optics for planetary imaging. Observers used modern tracking

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:56.940
mounts and cameras attached to the original tube assembly. Saturn's rings and cloud vans remain

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:03.060
visible despite the telescope's age. The image highlights how accessible planetary photography has

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:10.340
become even with legacy equipment. NASA and ESA facilities avoided major wildfire damage in Spain.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:17.440
Ground stations operated by both agencies escape serious harm from the fires. Assessment of any minor

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:23.620
effects will occur once access is restored. The site's support deep space communications for multiple

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:30.100
missions. Operations at the complexes continued without interruption during the event. Engineers will

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:36.360
inspect antennas and support buildings when safety permits. Before we go, keep an eye on upcoming

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:43.480
starship flight data that will decide whether the tower catch proceeds on flight 14. That covers today's

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:48.520
space and science news. Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting.

